      <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground mb-1">Direct Answer</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">For users 6'2" and taller, the Herman Miller Aeron Size C is the only viable fit. Size B's seat depth (~16.75"), seat height ceiling (~20"), and back height (~21.5") are all insufficient for tall users. Size C adds 1.5" of seat depth, a higher seat height maximum, and 1.75" more back height — the dimensions that matter for a 6'+ body.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-tca">

      <section>
        <h2>Size B vs Size C: All Three Dimensions That Matter</h2>
        <!-- tca-aio-capsule -->
        <p>TCA's Aeron size guide concludes that for users 6 feet 2 inches and taller, Size C is the only viable option. Size B's approximately 16.75-inch seat depth, 20-inch seat height ceiling, and 21.5-inch back height are all insufficient at that height. Size C adds roughly 1.5 inches of seat depth along with a higher seat height maximum and greater back height.</p>
        <p>
          Herman Miller designed each Aeron size with fixed dimensions calibrated to a target user range — there are no sliders
          that let you cross the size boundary after purchase. The differences between Size B and Size C affect three
          specifications that directly determine whether the chair fits a tall user.
        </p>
      </section>

